#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:12
msgid "Flow Control and Measurement Derivations"
msgstr "Control de flujo y derivaciones de medición"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:17
msgid ":math:`Q(t)` for a Tank with a Valve"
msgstr ":math:`Q(t)` para un tanque con válvula"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:18
msgid ""
"This document contains the derivation of the flow through a tank-"
"with-a-valve over time, :math:`Q(t)`. Our reference will be a simple "
"hypochlorinator, shown in the following image. In the image, a hypochlorite "
"solution is slowly dripping and mixing with piped source water, thereby "
"disinfecting it. The valve is almost closed to make sure that the "
"hypochlorite solution drips instead of flows. At the end of this document is"
" an image which shows the variables in the final equation."
msgstr ""
"Este documento contiene la derivación del flujo a través de un tanque con "
"válvula a lo largo del tiempo, :math:`Q(t)`. Nuestra referencia será un "
"hipoclorador simple, mostrado en la siguiente imagen. En la imagen, una "
"solución de hipoclorito gotea lentamente y se mezcla con agua fuente de "
"tubería, desinfectándola así. La válvula está casi cerrada para asegurarse "
"de que la solución de hipoclorito gotea en lugar de fluir. Al final de este "
"documento hay una imagen que muestra las variables en la ecuación final."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:27
msgid "Drip hypochlorinator"
msgstr "Hipoclorador por goteo"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:27
msgid ""
"This is a common setup for chlorinating water before distributing it to a "
"nearby community."
msgstr ""
"Esta es una configuración común para clorar agua antes de distribuirla a una"
" comunidad cercana."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:29
msgid ""
"This derivation begins by finding two equations for flow, :math:`Q`, through"
" the hypochlorinator and setting them equal to each other. First, the rate "
"of change of the volume of hypochlorite solution in the tank is equivalent "
"to the flow out of the hypochlorinator. Since the volume of hypochlorite "
"solution in the tank is equal to the tank’s cross-sectional area times it "
"height, we get the following equation:"
msgstr ""
"Esta derivación comienza encontrando dos ecuaciones para el flujo, "
":math:`Q`, a través del hipoclorador y igualándolas entre sí. Primero, la "
"tasa de cambio del volumen de la solución de hipoclorito en el tanque es "
"equivalente al flujo que sale del hipoclorador. Dado que el volumen de "
"solución de hipoclorito en el tanque es igual al área de la sección "
"transversal del tanque multiplicada por su altura, obtenemos la siguiente "
"ecuación:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:31
msgid "Q =  - \\frac{d\\forall}{dt} = - \\frac{{A_{Tank}}dh}{dt}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:35
#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:109
#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:139
#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:281
msgid "Such that:"
msgstr "Tal que:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:36
msgid ""
":math:`\\frac{d\\forall}{dt}` = rate of change in volume of solution in the "
"tank"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:37
msgid ""
":math:`\\frac{dh}{dt}` = rate of change in height of water (hypochlorite "
"solution) level with time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:39
msgid ""
"Our other equation for flow is the head loss equation. Since major losses "
"are negligible for a short pipe-low flow rate system, we only need to "
"consider minor losses. The only real minor loss in this system occurs in the"
" almost-closed valve that is dripping the hypochlorite solution. However, we"
" will also use the head loss trick. Therefore, the total driving head of the"
" system :math:`h` is equal to the minor losses:"
msgstr ""
"Nuestra otra ecuación para el flujo es la ecuación pérdida de carga . Dado "
"que las pérdidas mayores son insignificantes para un sistema de tubería "
"corta y bajo caudal , solo necesitamos considerar pérdidas menores. La única"
" pérdida menor real en este sistema ocurre en la válvula casi cerrada que "
"gotea la solución de hipoclorito. Sin embargo, también usaremos el truco "
"pérdida de carga . Por lo tanto, la carga total de impulso del sistema "
":math:`h` es igual a las pérdidas menores:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:41
msgid "h = h_e = \\left( \\sum K \\right) \\frac{Q^2}{2gA_{Valve}^2}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:45
msgid ""
"Bear in mind that this is the second form of the minor loss equation as "
"described in :ref:`this derivation <heading_final_minor_loss_equations>`. "
"Rearranging the minor loss equation to solve for :math:`Q`, it looks like "
"this:"
msgstr ""
"Ten en cuenta que esta es la segunda forma de la ecuación de pérdida menor "
"tal como se describe en :ref:`this derivation "
"<heading_final_minor_loss_equations>`. Reorganizando la ecuación de pérdida "
"menor para resolver :math:`Q`, se obtiene así:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:47
msgid "Q = A_{Valve} \\sqrt{\\frac{2 h_e g}{\\sum K}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:51
msgid ""
"Now we can set both equations for :math:`Q` equal to each other and move "
"them both to one side:"
msgstr ""
"Ahora podemos poner ambas ecuaciones para :math:`Q` iguales entre sí y "
"moverlas a un lado:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:53
msgid "A_{Tank} \\frac{dh}{dt} + A_{Valve} \\sqrt{\\frac{2gh}{\\sum K}} = 0"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:57
msgid ""
"From here, calculus and equation substitution dominate the derivation. "
"Separating the variables of the equation immediately above, we get the "
"following integral:"
msgstr ""
"A partir de aquí, el cálculo y la sustitución por ecuaciones dominan la "
"derivación. Separando las variables de la ecuación inmediatamente anterior, "
"obtenemos la siguiente integral:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:59
msgid ""
"\\frac{ -A_{Tank}}{{A_{Valve}} \\sqrt{\\frac{2g}{\\sum K}} }  \\int "
"\\limits_{h_0}^h \\frac{dh}{\\sqrt h} = \\int \\limits_0^t {dt}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:63
msgid "Which, when integrated, yields:"
msgstr "Lo que, al integrarse, da como resultado:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:65
msgid ""
"\\frac{ -A_{Tank}}{A_{Valve} \\sqrt{ \\frac{2g}{\\sum K}} } \\cdot 2 \\left("
" \\sqrt{h} - \\sqrt{h_0} \\right) = t"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:69
msgid "And solved for :math:`\\sqrt{h}` returns:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:71
msgid ""
"\\sqrt h  = \\sqrt{h_0} - t \\frac{A_{Valve}}{2 A_{tank}} \\sqrt "
"{\\frac{2g}{\\sum K}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:75
msgid ""
"At this point, the steps and equation substitutions may begin to seem "
"unintuitive. Do not worry if you do not understand why *exactly* a "
"particular substitution is occurring. Since we determined above that "
":math:`h_e = h`, our equation above for :math:`\\sqrt{h}` is also an "
"equation for :math:`\\sqrt{h_e}`. As such, we will plug the equation above "
"back into the minor loss equation solved for :math:`Q` from above, :math:`Q "
"= A_{Valve} \\sqrt{\\frac{2 h_e g}{\\sum K}}`, to produce:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:77
msgid ""
"Q = A_{Valve} \\sqrt{\\frac{2g}{\\sum K}} \\left( \\sqrt{h_0}  - t "
"\\frac{A_{Valve}}{2 A_{tank}} \\sqrt{\\frac{2g}{\\sum K}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:81
msgid ""
"Now we can focus on getting rid of the variables :math:`A_{Valve}`, "
":math:`\\sum K`, and :math:`A_{tank}`. By using the minor loss equation once"
" more, we can remove both :math:`A_{Valve}` and :math:`\\sum K`. Consider "
"the initial state of the system, when the hypochlorinator is set up and "
"starts dropping its first few drops of hypochlorite solution into the water."
" The initial flow rate, :math:`Q_0`, and elevation difference between the "
"water level and the valve, :math:`h_0`, can be input into the minor loss "
"equation, which can then be solved for :math:`A_{Valve}`:"
msgstr ""
"Ahora podemos centrarnos en eliminar las variables :math:`A_{Valve}`, "
":math:`\\sum K`, y :math:`A_{tank}`. Usando de nuevo la ecuación de pérdida "
"menor, podemos eliminar tanto :math:`A_{Valve}` como :math:`\\sum K`. "
"Consideremos el estado inicial del sistema, cuando el hipoclorador está "
"instalado y comienza a dejar caer sus primeras gotas de solución de "
"hipoclorito en el agua. La caudal inicial :math:`Q_0`, y la diferencia de "
"elevación entre el nivel del agua y la válvula, :math:`h_0`, pueden "
"introducirse en la ecuación de pérdida menor, que luego puede resolverse "
"para :math:`A_{Valve}`:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:83
msgid "A_{Valve} = \\frac{Q_{0}}{ \\sqrt{ \\frac{2 h_0 g}{\\sum K}} }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:87
msgid ""
"Plugging this equation for :math:`A_{Valve}` into the equation for :math:`Q`"
" just above, we get the following two equations, in which the second "
"equation is a simplified version of the first:"
msgstr ""
"Introduciendo esta ecuación para :math:`A_{Valve}` en la ecuación para "
":math:`Q` justo arriba, obtenemos las siguientes dos ecuaciones, en las que "
"la segunda ecuación es una versión simplificada de la primera:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:89
msgid ""
"Q = Q_0 \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{h_0}} \\left( \\sqrt{h_0} - \\frac{Q_0 t}{2 "
"A_{Tank} \\sqrt{h_0}} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:93
msgid "\\frac{Q}{Q_0} = 1 - \\frac{t Q_0}{2 A_{Tank} h_0}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:97
msgid ""
"This next step will eliminate :math:`A_{Tank}`. However, it requires some "
"clever manipulation that has a tendency to cause some confusion. We will "
"define a new parameter, :math:`t_{Design}`, which represents the time it "
"would take to empty the tank if the initial flow rate through the valve, "
":math:`Q_0`, stays constant in time. Of course, the flow :math:`Q` through "
"the valve does not stay constant in time, which is why this derivation "
"document exists. But imagining this hypothetical :math:`t_{Design}` "
"parameter allows us to form the following equation:"
msgstr ""
"Este siguiente paso eliminará :math:`A_{Tank}`. Sin embargo, requiere una "
"manipulación ingeniosa que tiende a causar cierta confusión. Definiremos un "
"nuevo parámetro, :math:`t_{Design}`, que representa el tiempo que tardaría "
"en vaciar el depósito si la caudal inicial a través de la válvula, "
":math:`Q_0`, se mantiene constante en el tiempo. Por supuesto, el flujo "
":math:`Q` a través de la válvula no se mantiene constante en el tiempo, por "
"lo que existe este documento de derivación. Pero imaginar este parámetro "
"hipotético :math:`t_{Design}` nos permite formar la siguiente ecuación:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:99
msgid "Q_0 t_{Design} = A_{Tank} h_{Tank}"
msgstr "Q_0 t_{Design} = A_{Tank} h_{Tank}"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:103
msgid ""
"This equation describes draining all the hypochlorite solution from the "
"tank. The volume of the solution, :math:`A_{Tank} h_{Tank}`, is drained in "
":math:`t_{Design}`. Rearranged, the equation becomes:"
msgstr ""
"Esta ecuación describe el drenaje de toda la solución de hipoclorito del "
"tanque. El volumen de la solución, :math:`A_{Tank} h_{Tank}`, se drena en "
":math:`t_{Design}`. Reordenada, la ecuación se convierte en:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:105
msgid "\\frac{Q_0}{A_{Tank}} = \\frac{h_{Tank}}{t_{Design}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:110
msgid ""
":math:`h_{Tank}` = elevation of water level in the tank with reference to "
"tank bottom at the initial state, :math:`t = 0`"
msgstr ""
":math:`h_{Tank}` = elevación de nivel del agua en el tanque en referencia al"
" fondo del tanque en el estado inicial, :math:`t = 0`"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:112
msgid ""
"Here lies another common source of confusion. :math:`h_{Tank}` is not the "
"same as :math:`h_{0}`. :math:`h_{Tank}` is the height of water level in the "
"tank with reference to the tank bottom. :math:`h_{0}` is the water level in "
"the tank with reference to the valve. Therefore, :math:`h_{0} \\geq "
"h_{Tank}` is true if the valve is located at or below the bottom of the "
"tank. If the tank is elevated far above the valve, then the :math:`h_{0} > >"
" h_{Tank}`. If the valve is at the same elevation as the bottom of the tank,"
" then :math:`h_{0} = h_{Tank}`. Please refer to the following image to "
"clarify :math:`h_{0}` and :math:`h_{Tank}`. Also note that both "
":math:`h_{Tank}` and :math:`h_{0}` are not variables, they are constants "
"which are defined by the initial state of the hypochlorinator, when the "
"solution just begins to flow."
msgstr ""
"Aquí reside otra fuente común de confusión. :math:`h_{Tank}` no es lo mismo "
"que :math:`h_{0}`. :math:`h_{Tank}` es la altura de nivel del agua en el "
"depósito en referencia al fondo del tanque. :math:`h_{0}` es la nivel del "
"agua en el depósito en referencia a la válvula. Por lo tanto, :math:`h_{0} "
"\\geq h_{Tank}` es cierto si la válvula está situada en o por debajo del "
"fondo del tanque. Si el depósito está elevado mucho por encima de la "
"válvula, entonces el :math:`h_{0} > > h_{Tank}`. Si la válvula está a la "
"misma elevación que el fondo del tanque, entonces :math:`h_{0} = h_{Tank}`. "
"Por favor, consulte la siguiente imagen para aclarar :math:`h_{0}` y "
":math:`h_{Tank}`. También hay que notar que tanto :math:`h_{Tank}` como "
":math:`h_{0}` no son variables, son constantes definidas por el estado "
"inicial del hipoclorador, cuando la solución comienza a fluir."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:121
msgid "Hypochlorinator variables"
msgstr "Variables hipocloradoras"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:121
msgid ""
":math:`Q_0 =` initial flow rate of hypochlorite solution at time :math:`t = "
"0`, :math:`t_{Design} =` time it would take to drain the tank if flow was "
"held constant at :math:`Q_0`"
msgstr ""
":math:`Q_0 =` caudal inicial de solución de hipoclorito en el tiempo "
":math:`t = 0` :math:`t_{Design} =` tiempo que tardaría en vaciar el tanque "
"si el flujo se mantenía constante en :math:`Q_0`"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:123
msgid ""
"Finally, our fabricated equivalence, :math:`\\frac{Q_0}{A_{Tank}} = "
"\\frac{h_{Tank}}{t_{Design}}` can be plugged into :math:`\\frac{Q}{Q_0} = 1 "
"- \\frac{t Q_0}{2 A_{Tank} h_0}` to create the highly useful equation for "
"flow rate as a function of time for a drip hypochlorinator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:125
msgid "\\frac{Q}{Q_0} = 1 - \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{t}{t_{Design}} \\frac{h_{Tank}}{h_0}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:132
msgid "Which can be slightly rearranged to yield:"
msgstr "Que puede reorganizarse ligeramente para dar como resultado:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:134
msgid ""
"Q(t) = Q_0 \\left( 1 - \\frac{1}{2} \\frac{t}{t_{Design}} "
"\\frac{h_{Tank}}{h_0} \\right)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:140
msgid ":math:`Q = Q(t)` = flow of hypochlorite through valve at time :math:`t`"
msgstr ""
":math:`Q = Q(t)` = flujo de hipoclorito a través de la válvula en el tiempo "
":math:`t`"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:141
msgid ":math:`t` = elapsed time"
msgstr ":math:`t` = tiempo transcurrido"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:142
msgid ""
":math:`t_{Design}` = time it would take for tank to empty *if* flow stayed "
"constant at :math:`Q_0`, which it does not"
msgstr ""
":math:`t_{Design}` = tiempo que tardaría en vaciar el tanque *si* el flujo "
"se mantuviera constante en :math:`Q_0`, que no ocurre"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:143
msgid ":math:`h_{Tank}` = elevation of water level with reference to tank bottom"
msgstr ""
":math:`h_{Tank}` = elevación de nivel del agua en referencia al fondo del "
"tanque"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:144
msgid ":math:`h_0` = elevation of water level with reference to the valve"
msgstr ":math:`h_0` = elevación de nivel del agua respecto a la válvula"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:146
msgid ""
"“How does this ‘tank with a valve’ scenario differ from the ‘hole in a "
"bucket’ scenario?” some might ask. If you are interested, you may go through"
" the derivation on your own using the orifice equation instead of the minor "
"loss equation for the first step. If you do so you’ll find that the equation"
" remains almost the same, the only difference being that the "
":math:`\\frac{h_{Tank}}{h_0}` term drops out for an orifice, as "
":math:`h_{Tank} = h_0`. The big difference in the systems lies with the "
"flexibility of having a valve. It can be tightened or loosened to change the"
" flow rate, whereas changing the size of an orifice multiple times in a row "
"is not recommended and is usually irreversible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:154
msgid ":math:`D(t)` and :math:`t(D)` for Tank Drain Equation"
msgstr ":math:`D(t)` y :math:`t(D)` para la ecuación de drenaje del tanque"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:155
msgid ""
"This document contains the derivation of :math:`D_{Pipe}`, which is the pipe"
" diameter necessary to install in a drain system to entirely drain a tank in"
" time :math:`t_{Drain}`."
msgstr ""
"Este documento contiene la derivación de :math:`D_{Pipe}`, que es el "
"diámetro de tubería necesario para instalar en un sistema de drenaje y así "
"vaciar completamente un depósito en el tiempo :math:`t_{Drain}`."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:157
msgid ""
"First, it is necessary to understand how AguaClara tank drains work and what"
" they look like. Many tanks, including the flocculator and entrance tank, "
"have a hole in their bottoms which are fitted with pipe couplings. During "
"normal operation, these couplings have pipe stubs in them, and the pipe "
"stubs are tall enough to go above the water level in the tank and not allow "
"water to flow into the drain. When the pipe stub is removed, the water "
"begins to flow out of the drain, as the image below indicates. The drain "
"pipe consists of pipe and one elbow, shown in the image."
msgstr ""
"Primero, es necesario entender cómo funcionan los desagües de los depósitos "
"AguaClara y cómo son físicamente. Muchos tanques, incluyendo el floculador y"
" el tanque de entrada, tienen un agujero en el fondo equipado con "
"acoplamientos para tuberías. Durante operación normal, estos acoplamientos "
"tienen muñones de tubería, y los muñones son lo suficientemente altos como "
"para superar la nivel del agua del depósito y no permitir que el agua fluya "
"hacia el desagüe. Cuando se retira el muñón de la tubería, el agua comienza "
"a salir del desagüe, como indica la imagen de abajo. El tubo de desagüe "
"consta de tubería y un codo, como se muestra en la imagen."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:166
msgid "Pipe stub drainage"
msgstr "Drenaje de tubajes"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:166
msgid "This is AguaClara's alternatives to having valves."
msgstr "Esta es la alternativa de AguaClara a tener válvulas."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:168
msgid ""
"While AguaClara clarifiers use valves instead of pipe to begin the process "
"of draining, the actual drain piping system is the same, pipe and an elbow. "
"The equation that will soon be derived applies to both pipe stub and valve "
"drains."
msgstr ""
"Mientras que los clarificadores AguaClara utilizan válvulas en lugar de "
"tubería para iniciar el proceso de drenaje, el sistema real de tuberías de "
"drenaje es el mismo: tubería y codo. La ecuación que pronto se derivará se "
"aplica tanto al tubo de tubería como a los drenajes de válvulas."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:170
msgid ""
"We will start the derivation from the following equation, which is found in "
"an intermediate step from the ':math:`Q(t)` "
":ref:`heading_flow_for_a_tank_with_a_valve`.' While this system does not "
"have a valve, it has other sources of minor loss and therefore the equation "
"is still valid."
msgstr ""
"Comenzaremos la derivación a partir de la siguiente ecuación, que se "
"encuentra en un paso intermedio desde el ':math:`Q(t)` "
":ref:`heading_flow_for_a_tank_with_a_valve`.' Aunque este sistema no tiene "
"válvula, tiene otras fuentes de pérdida menor y, por tanto, la ecuación "
"sigue siendo válida."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:172
msgid ""
"\\sqrt h  = \\sqrt{h_0} - t \\frac{A_{Valve}}{2 A_{Tank}} \\sqrt "
"{\\frac{2g}{K}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:176
msgid ""
"We need to make some adjustments to this equation before proceeding, to make"
" it applicable for this new drain-system scenario. First, we want to assume "
"that the tank has fully drained. Thus, :math:`t = t_{Drain}` and :math:`h = "
"0`. Next, we recall that the tank drain is not actually a valve, but just "
"pipe and an elbow, so :math:`A_{Valve} = A_{Pipe}`. Additionally, there can "
"be multiple points of minor loss in the drain system: the entrance from the "
"tank into the drain pipe, the elbow, and potentially the exit of the water "
"out of the drain pipe. When considering a clarifier, the open valve required"
" to begin drainage also has a minor loss associated with it. Therefore, it "
"is necessary to substitute :math:`\\sum K` for :math:`K` With these "
"substitutions, the equation becomes:"
msgstr ""
"Necesitamos hacer algunos ajustes en esta ecuación antes de continuar, para "
"que sea aplicable a este nuevo escenario de sistema de drenaje. Primero, "
"queremos asumir que el depósito ha vaciado completamente. Así, :math:`t = "
"t_{Drain}` y :math:`h = 0`. A continuación, recordamos que el desagüe del "
"tanque no es realmente una válvula, sino solo tubería y un codo, así que "
":math:`A_{Valve} = A_{Pipe}`. Además, puede haber múltiples puntos de "
"pérdida menor en el sistema de drenaje: la entrada del depósito a la tubería"
" de drenaje, el codo y, potencialmente, la salida del agua por la tubería de"
" drenaje. Al considerar un clarificador, la válvula abierta necesaria para "
"iniciar el drenaje también tiene una pérdida menor asociada. Por lo tanto, "
"es necesario sustituir :math:`\\sum K` por :math:`K` Con estas "
"sustituciones, la ecuación se convierte en:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:178
msgid ""
"\\sqrt{h_0}  = t_{Drain} \\frac{A_{Pipe}}{2 A_{Tank}} \\sqrt "
"{\\frac{2g}{\\sum K}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:182
msgid ""
"Now, with the knowledge that :math:`A_{Pipe} = \\frac{\\pi D_{Pipe}^2}{4}` "
"and rearranging to solve for :math:`D_{Pipe}`, we obtain the following "
"equation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:184
msgid ""
"D_{Pipe} = \\sqrt{ \\frac{8 A_{Tank}}{\\pi t_{Drain}} \\sqrt{ \\frac{h_0 "
"\\sum K}{2g} } }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:188
msgid ""
"To get the equation in terms of easily measureable tank parameters, we "
"substitute :math:`L_{Tank} W_{Tank}` for :math:`A_{Tank}`. To maintain "
"consistency in variable names, we substitute :math:`H_{Tank}` for "
":math:`h_0`."
msgstr ""
"Para obtener la ecuación en términos de parámetros de tanque fácilmente "
"medibles, sustituimos :math:`L_{Tank} W_{Tank}` por :math:`A_{Tank}`. Para "
"mantener la consistencia en los nombres de variables, sustituimos "
":math:`H_{Tank}` por :math:`h_0`."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:190
msgid ""
"By saying that :math:`h_0 = H_{Tank}`, we are making the assumption that the"
" pipe drain is at the same elevation as the bottom of the tank. The pipe "
"drain is actually a little lower than the bottom of the tank, but that would"
" make the tank drain faster than :math:`t_{Drain}`, which is preferred. "
"Therefore, we are designing a slight safety factor when we say that "
":math:`h_0 = H_{Tank}`."
msgstr ""
"Al decir que :math:`h_0 = H_{Tank}`, asumimos que el desagüe de la tubería "
"está a la misma altitud que el fondo del tanque. El desagüe de la tubería "
"está en realidad un poco más bajo que el fondo del tanque, pero eso haría "
"que el depósito drene más rápido que :math:`t_{Drain}`, que es lo preferido."
" Por lo tanto, estamos diseñando un pequeño factor de seguridad cuando "
"decimos que :math:`h_0 = H_{Tank}`."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:192
msgid "Finally, we arrive at the equation for drain pipe sizing:"
msgstr "Finalmente, llegamos a la ecuación para el tamaño de las tuberías de drenaje:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:194
msgid ""
"D_{Pipe} = \\sqrt{ \\frac{8 L_{Tank} W_{Tank}}{\\pi t_{Drain}}} \\left( "
"\\frac{H_{Tank} \\sum K}{2g} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{4}}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:198
msgid ""
"We can also easily rearrange to find the time required to drain a tank given"
" a drain diameter:"
msgstr ""
"También podemos reorganizar fácilmente para encontrar el tiempo necesario "
"para vaciar un depósito dado un diámetro de drenaje:"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:200
msgid ""
"t_{Drain} = \\frac{8 L_{Tank} W_{Tank}}{\\pi D_{Pipe}^2} \\sqrt{ "
"\\frac{H_{Tank} \\sum K}{2g} }"
msgstr ""

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:204
msgid "Such that the variables are as the appear in the image below."
msgstr "De modo que las variables sean como aparecen en la imagen de abajo."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:213
msgid "Pipe stub drainage variables"
msgstr "Variables de drenaje de tubajes"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:213
msgid ""
":math:`L_{Tank}` is the length of the tank which goes the page. :math:`K` is"
" the aggregate minor loss coefficient of the drain system."
msgstr ""
":math:`L_{Tank}` es la longitud del depósito que recorre la página. "
":math:`K` es el coeficiente de pérdida menor agregado del sistema de "
"drenaje."

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:218
msgid "Kinematic Viscosity of Coagulants"
msgstr "Viscosidad cinemática de los coagulantes"

#: ../../Flow_Control_and_Measurement/FCM_Derivations.rst:220
msgid ""
"AguaClara plants currently use either alum (aluminum sulfate) or PACl "
"(polyaluminum chloride) solutions that are prepared from granular chemicals."
" The viscosity of solutions created from granular alum and PACl were "
"measured using a SV - 10 Vibro Viscometer. The results are shown below.   "
"`Colab workspace  plotting the viscosity of alum and PACl "
"<https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1fWZQ-"
"BsXeINM31NgzbJO2Piv7NZug0cI#scrollTo=_YAf5yN3P0TE>`_."
msgstr ""
"Las plantas de AguaClara utilizan actualmente soluciones de alumbre (sulfato"
" de aluminio) o PACl (cloruro de polialuminio) preparadas a partir de "
"productos químicos granulares. La viscosidad de las soluciones creadas a "
"partir de alumbre granular y PACl se midió usando un viscosímetro vibro "
"SV-10. Los resultados se muestran a continuación. `Espacio de trabajo de "
"Colab que representa la viscosidad del alumbre y el PACl "
"<https://colab.research.google.com/drive/1fWZQ-"
